# TypeScript best practices

## Types

Prefer `interface` for object shapes (props, options, DTOs):

```ts
// ❌ BAD
type ItemOptions = { name: string; limit?: number };

// ✅ GOOD
interface ItemOptions {
  name: string;
  limit?: number;
}
```

Prefer `type` for inferred schema types, intersections, unions, and aliases:

```ts
// ❌ BAD — hand-duplicating a schema / forcing a union into an interface
interface Item {
  name: string;
}
interface Status {
  kind: 'idle' | 'loading' | 'error';
}

// ✅ GOOD
type Item = z.infer<typeof itemSchema>;
type Status = 'idle' | 'loading' | 'error';
type ItemWithId = Item & { id: string };
```

Colocate types with the module; export only what other packages need:

```ts
// ❌ BAD — barrel re-exports every private helper type
export type { InternalParseState, ItemOptions };

// ✅ GOOD — export the public contract only
interface InternalParseState {
  /* ... */
}
export interface ItemOptions {
  name: string;
}
```

## Validation

Public / untrusted input is `unknown`; validate with a schema at the boundary:

```ts
// ❌ BAD
async function addItem(input: any) {
  return store.add(input as Item);
}

// ✅ GOOD
async function addItem(input: unknown): Promise<Item[]> {
  const item = itemSchema.parse(input);
  return store.add(item);
}
```

Own the schema next to the boundary; derive types from the schema:

```ts
// ❌ BAD — hand-written type drifts from runtime checks
interface Item {
  name: string;
}
function addItem(input: Item) {
  /* no parse */
}

// ✅ GOOD
const itemSchema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1) });
type Item = z.infer<typeof itemSchema>;
```

Prefer throw-on-invalid over Result / `safeParse` for domain APIs:

```ts
// ❌ BAD
const parsed = itemSchema.safeParse(input);
if (!parsed.success) return { ok: false, error: parsed.error };

// ✅ GOOD
const item = itemSchema.parse(input);
```

## Control flow

Prefer early returns / guard clauses over deep nesting:

```ts
// ❌ BAD
function label(item?: Item) {
  if (item) {
    if (item.name) {
      return item.name;
    }
  }
  return 'unknown';
}

// ✅ GOOD
function label(item?: Item) {
  if (!item?.name) return 'unknown';
  return item.name;
}
```

## Type-only imports

Use `import type` or inline `type` specifiers for type-only imports:

```ts
// ❌ BAD
import { CatalogItem, createContext, ReactNode } from '...';

// ✅ GOOD
import type { CatalogItem } from '../types/catalog';
import { createContext, type ReactNode } from 'react';
```

## Avoid

No `enum` or `namespace` — prefer string literal unions and schemas:

```ts
// ❌ BAD
enum Status {
  Idle,
  Loading
}
namespace Items {
  export type Id = string;
}

// ✅ GOOD
type Status = 'idle' | 'loading';
type ItemId = string;
```

## React

Name props `ComponentNameProps`; colocate; usually leave unexported.
Use `function Component(props: Props)` — not `React.FC`:

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
const ListItem: React.FC<{ item: Item }> = ({ item }) => <li>{item.name}</li>;

// ✅ GOOD
interface ListItemProps {
  item: Item;
}

export function ListItem({ item }: ListItemProps) {
  return <li>{item.name}</li>;
}
```
